# Inspector

- `internal/inspector.Service` owns the installation's Golem Inspector. The hub is a managed container (`docker.HubSpec`, name `golem-deploy_inspector`, label `inspector-hub=true`, found with `Client.InspectorHubs`/`IsInspectorHub` by labels and name, never by a client value). Only the daemon talks to it, over its `127.0.0.1` port with the fixed origin `HubOrigin` and host `hubHost`. Never embed the hub package, never change the engine, never put the hub on a network shared with games (`EnsureNetwork` not isolated fails the apply and removes a hub there).
- The hub port (`hubContainer`/`hubPort` under `mu`) is valid only for that container while Docker reports it running and publishing that port: `refreshHub` (coalesced through `flight`, at most every `hubRefreshMin`) clears it otherwise, `hubUnreachable` drops it on any transport error (`transportError`) and kicks a refresh, and `hubLogin` runs `verifyHub` (Docker, cached `hubVerifyTTL`) before sending the password. Requests and relays use one `hubView` snapshot (generation, secrets, container, port) from start to end. `mu` is never held across a Docker or hub call; the network addresses and the target list are refreshed through `flight`.
- Admission: tickets, sessions and agent relays are registered only through `admit(gen, ...)` under `mu` while `admitting` is open with the generation the request started with. `endAdmission` (turn-off, shutdown) and a rotation change `gen` before `drain`, so nothing admitted before survives it.
- Hub secrets (`hubSecrets`: password and its cost-12 bcrypt hash, hub agent token, per-server token key, generation) are generated on first enable or `rotate_secrets`, sealed with `secrets.InspectorSecretsPurpose` in `settings.inspector_secrets` (durable write) and only reach the hub's env file. Never return, log or put them in argv or labels; config hashes use the generation. Per-server tokens are `gdi_<id>_<hex HMAC-SHA256>` (`agentToken`, constant-time `verifyAgentToken`).
- `inspector.apply` (restricted, subject `inspector/hub`). `Update` answers `ErrUnavailable` (409) with `--inspector-listen off`, validates against the stored setting, writes `settings.inspector_enabled` first (`storeEnabled`; a failed write fails the request; `settingMu` keeps the copy in `enabled` behind the database), then starts the job. The job reads the setting when it runs (`loadEnabled`): off or unavailable removes everything (`disable`); on runs image (`ResolveImage`; `PullImage` with the build client configuration only when missing or `pull_image`; the automatic repair never pulls) before anything changes, `EnsureNetwork`, secrets (a rotation stores them, bumps `gen`, forgets the old hub and drains), keep a running hub whose `config-hash` label and image match `hubHash` or replace it, `waitHubReady`, daemon login, open the listener and admission. Any failure calls `stopServing` (hub forgotten, state `stopped`, user-safe `detail`); Docker stderr goes to the daemon log only; hub output goes to the job log through `logsafe` with `hub | ` and secrets redacted. `Run` starts the repair at start when the Inspector is on, or when a hub exists that should not (`hubLeft`), then at most every `RepairInterval` after an attempt (`needsRepair`); it sweeps sessions and agent relays every `SweepInterval` and keeps the hub port current; `Close` ends everything at shutdown (the listener waits for relays within its grace), the hub keeps running.
- The listener is bound only while the setting is on. With panel TLS it serves the panel certificate to browsers and plaintext to agents on one port (`sniffer`, first byte 0x16, bounded wait; closing it closes waiting sockets); without TLS one plaintext server does both. `limitListener` caps open connections (`maxConns`) for their whole life; `DisableGeneralOptionsHandler` is set. Every handler runs under the listener's `tracker`; relayed streams get their client connection through `proxyWriter.Hijack` (deadlines cleared, `trackedConn` closed when the request context ends, CloseWrite hidden), and `proxyWriter` drops the hub's 1xx responses and trailers. `prepare` starts every request: read and write deadlines (`reqTimeout`), and refuses announced trailers, GET/HEAD with a body, upgrades other than WebSocket and upgrades with a body; `reject` adds `Connection: close` and a short drain for requests with a body. Never rewrite a request's `Body` or `ContentLength`. Browser requests use the shared `netpolicy.Policy` (Host allowlist plus the `--inspector-public-url` host, `ClientIP`). The agent endpoint skips the Host policy and checks the peer instead (managed network subnets; loopback only off Linux or with `--inspector-agent-host`) and refuses `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For` and `Via`.
- Browser side: no cookie is set or read on the listener (game servers share the host name on other ports); the session key lives in the tab's `sessionStorage` of the Inspector's origin. One-time tickets (32 random bytes, hashed, 60 s, bounded per account and in total) from `CreateSession` travel only in the fragment of `EnterPath` (`#t=`); the target stays bound to the ticket. The daemon's own paths (`ownPrefix`, `browser.go`): the static entry page (CSP `script-src 'self'`, `connect-src 'self'`, style by hash), `enter.js`, `boot.js` (embedded ES5/ASCII files, tested in jsdom by `frontend/src/test/inspector-scripts.test.ts`), the ended page, and `POST SessionPath` (Origin, JSON content type, the failure limiter before the body, 1 KiB; consumes the ticket, checks the account, logs in at the hub, registers the session through `admit`, answers `{key, target_id}` no-store; only the key's hash is kept). Every hub HTML response gets `bootTag` after its leading `<head>` tag (`headEnd`: only whitespace, comments, a doctype and an `<html>` tag may come first; identity encoding, 2 MiB, Content-Length fixed; anything else, larger, compressed or 206 is a 502 page). `boot.js` resolves a URL once against the document's base URL and passes exactly the checked URL (or a native copy of a Request) to the native call. `/api`, `/auth`, `/ws` need a session presented as `Authorization: Bearer` or, on upgrades, the subprotocols `WebSocketProtocol` + `WebSocketKeyPrefix<key>` (the 101 then selects the marker); otherwise 401 `auth_required` with `SessionHeader: ended`. Only WebSocket upgrades under `/ws/` are relayed. Other GET/HEAD paths are the static UI, relayed with no credentials and no session lookup. A proxied request checks the key in memory, then the Origin, reads its body, then rechecks the account (exists, approved, same token version) and registers with the session in one step (`session.track`, which refuses an ended or expired session and gives the request a deadline at the expiry); 8 h lifetime; sign-out (POST only) ends the session locally, then logs out at the hub. The proxy strips cookies, credentials, subprotocols and forwarding headers, injects only the session's hub cookie, rewrites Origin, drops `Set-Cookie` and `SessionHeader`, never forwards `blockedPaths` (`/auth/login`, `/ws/agent`, `/metrics`, `/health`, `/live`, `/ready`), and requires exactly one Origin, the browser's own in canonical form (`canonicalOrigin`), for WebSockets and non-GET/HEAD. Never log the key, `Authorization` or `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`. A lost hub session (401, or `/auth/session` false) is renewed once per request through `relogin`; when that fails the request is a 503 (`errHubSessionLost`), an `/auth/session` answer over 4 KiB a 502, and a request whose context ended still gets an answer. Hub logins are serialised (`loginSem`); `loginGuard` holds logins per hub generation (container and secrets' generation) after a 401 (for good) or 429 (until Retry-After), never shortens a hold and resets only when the generation changes. Ended sessions cancel their tracked requests, which closes open WebSocket streams on both sides.
- Agent side: relay `/ws/agent` with the hub's token instead of the server's, one relay per server (a new valid connection closes the previous one) and at most `MaxAgents` in total; `agents.closeAll` on off and rotation; `sweepAgents` closes relays of deleted servers and servers whose switch is off.
- `Agent` implements `servers.Inspector`: endpoint, generation and a token function of that generation in one snapshot, following the desired configuration (not whether the hub runs). The servers rule covers injection and the config hash. `GET /api/v1/inspector` (`Status`) reads the hub container and, through a daemon-held hub session, `/api/targets` (cached `targetsTTL`, bounded body, only bounded fields relayed, matched by server name among servers with the switch on).
- Verify with `go test ./internal/inspector/... ./internal/netpolicy/... ./internal/httpapi/...` (unit tests use `testHooks` for the races and a fake hub whose `inspect` stands in for Docker); with Docker, `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/inspector/` builds a stand-in hub image (`inspectortest/standinhub`), applies it for real, checks hardening and labels, serves the static UI with the boot script, exchanges a ticket, proxies a request and a browser WebSocket with the key and relays an agent from a container. Changes to `boot.js` or `enter.js` also need `npm test` in `frontend/`.
